From d9170b0a3bf66afd42d007fd896fe942b9086d5b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Craig Johnson <1545411+captainpacket@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 03:22:40 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] reconcile: see through KubeVirt's placeholder dummy and
 repair the wire the VM uses

Two halves of one defect: the reconciler never repaired a broken wire on
a KubeVirt VM pod, and when anything did try, the "repair" built a wire
the VM could not reach.

THE LAYOUT (measured, virt-launcher-siteE-rr-rt02, example-lab-shared
2026-09-21). After bridge binding every healthy VM link is: k6t-<id>
bridge, tap<id> and <id>-nic (our vxlan, renamed) enslaved to it, and a
DUMMY left under the original pod<id> name as virt-launcher's
placeholder. The original name is therefore OCCUPIED on every bound VM
pod -- by something that is not the wire.

HALF ONE: reconcilePod set evidence.RenamedFormPresent from the
attribute-blind name-presence probe whenever the inspector said not-OK,
and classifyWire consults that flag before the healthBroken -> repair
branch. The check now gates the same-node veth path only, where koko's
inspector genuinely cannot see through the rename. (Live: skipped=12
[renamed-form] -> skipped=2, the two same-node veths.)

HALF TWO, which half one alone exposed (live: the same ten wires moved
to skipped [wire-state-could-not-be-established]): vxlan.Inspect looked
the wire up under its original name, found the dummy, and classifyLink
said "occupied by a *netlink.Dummy" -- unknown, so nothing to repair.
0013's renamed-form lookup only ran when the original name was ABSENT,
which on a bound VM pod it never is. CreateOrUpdate had the identical
blind spot with a worse outcome: it deleted the dummy and built a fresh
vxlan under pod<id>, outside the bridge -- the same pod shows exactly
that (pod63c8fdd4323 bare, k6t-63c8fdd4323 holding only the tap) plus a
stray pod151b6d47576 beside a correct 151b6d47576-nic.

resolveSlot (daemon/vxlan/kubevirt_slot.go) now answers, for both
Inspect and CreateOrUpdate, which link carries the wire (<id>-nic when
KubeVirt has bound it), what name a rebuilt one must take, which bridge
it must join, and which bare vxlans under the original name are strays.
classifySlot adds two positive-evidence verdicts: a vxlan outside its
bridge is broken, a stray beside the bridged wire is broken. The repair
removes strays and the found link under the names they were found as,
creates the vxlan under the "-nic" name, and enslaves it to the bridge.
The dummy is never touched; KubeVirt owns it. A dummy with neither
bridge nor "-nic" stays unknown. Non-KubeVirt wires (eth1) and a VM pod
whose binding has not run resolve exactly as before.

Tests: the reconcile cross-node regression pair (renamed form present +
broken -> repaired; + OK -> healthy) and seven resolveSlot/classifySlot
cases modelled on the live link table, including the bare-outside-bridge
and stray shapes. kubevirtRenamedLink is superseded and removed.
